<p>Iran said the Strait of Hormuz is open to commercial shipping for the rest of the ceasefire, and vessel-tracking data showed the first notable convoy of tankers moving through the waterway on April 18. That reopening brought immediate relief to markets, with oil prices falling sharply after weeks of disruption at one of the world’s most important energy chokepoints. Even so, the situation remains fragile. President Trump said the US naval blockade on Iranian ports will stay in place unless Washington and Tehran reach a broader agreement, and he warned the ceasefire due to expire next week might not be extended without a deal. Iranian officials responded that the strait could close again if the blockade continues, while shipping companies, insurers and naval advisers said mines, security procedures and mixed political signals still make a full return to normal traffic far from certain.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/YC5kyG9ZkLYFmLyPnsXtQFI7uni74WNxvMMrbKGpsoX6C3wII9QOPy29hTUrZA48oFBKJbz23dkcE5XgNAkl0mUFJqbBpwBaaChvsOiLOIuc' alt='A drone view shows the Malta-flagged Agios Fanourios-I, an oil tanker that sailed through the Strait of Hormuz, arriving in Iraq’s territorial waters off Basra.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Route controls: Iranian officials said ships can pass only through coordinated corridors and must get approval from the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps, adding a new layer of control even after the reopening announcement.</li><li>False starts: Some tankers trying to cross on April 17 turned back or reversed course amid confusion over the strait’s status, showing that the reopening announcement did not immediately resolve operational doubts.</li><li>Deal linkage: A senior Iranian official said unfreezing Iranian funds was part of the arrangement, suggesting that maritime access is tied not just to security terms but to wider financial bargaining as well.</li><li>No-tolls signal: Trump said there were &quot;not going to be tolls&quot; imposed by Iran on ships using Hormuz, addressing one specific fear that surfaced in earlier peace-deal discussions.</li></ul><blockquote>Maybe I won’t extend it, but the blockade (on Iranian ports) is going to remain - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>United States: President Trump cast the reopening as a sign of progress and said a broader agreement with Iran could come quickly, while keeping pressure on Tehran by insisting the blockade would remain until a deal is complete. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/trump-says-he-has-good-news-iran-no-clarity-peace-deal'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>Iran: Iranian leaders described the strait’s reopening as conditional, saying commercial traffic can resume during the ceasefire but warning that continued US pressure or blockade measures could lead to another closure. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/17/strait-of-hormuz-now-open-to-commercial-vessels'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Shipping industry: Shipping firms welcomed the announcement cautiously but said they still needed clarity on mines, routing rules and Iranian conditions before sending more vessels through the chokepoint. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/4/17/shipping-firms-seek-clarifications-before-crossing-hormuz'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Insurers: Marine insurers said they were waiting to see more ships transit safely before cutting war-risk premiums, treating pricing as a real-world test of whether the waterway is truly secure. (<a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/17/nx-s1-5785167/hormuz-reopens-but-insurers-arent-ready-to-sound-the-all-clear'>NPR</a>)</li><li>Australia: Prime Minister Anthony Albanese stressed that Asia and the Indo-Pacific are feeling the disruption acutely because most oil that normally moves through Hormuz is bound for the region, giving Australia a direct economic stake in restoring secure passage. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/australia-world-leaders-warily-welcome-strait-opening/'>Michael West</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/trump-says-he-has-good-news-on-iran-no-clarity-on-peace-deal'>Trump says he has &#x27;good news&#x27; on Iran as tankers cross Strait of Hormuz</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/video-what-you-need-to-know-as-us-and-iran-say-strait-of-hormuz-open-to/'>What you need to know as U.S. and Iran say Strait of Hormuz open to commercial ships</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/04/trump-says-he-has-good-news-iran-no-clarity-peace-deal'>Trump says he has &#x27;good news&#x27; on Iran, no clarity on peace deal</a> - al-monitor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/04/17/2026/the-hormuz-crisis-is-far-from-over'>The Hormuz crisis is far from over</a> - semafor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/live-updates/iran-war-trump-israel-lebanon-ceasefire-hezbollah-strait-hormuz/'>Trump says U.S. will continue blockade after Iran says strait &quot;completely open&quot;</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1992831/trump-says-us-blockade-of-iranian-ports-will-remain-if-no-deal-reached'>Trump says US blockade of Iranian ports will ‘remain’ if no deal reached</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rferl.org/a/iran-war-us-israel-lebanon-hormuz/33734636.html'>Trump Says US-Iran Deal &#x27;Very Close&#x27; After Tehran Declares Strait Of Hormuz Open</a> - rferl.org</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ckg045z73z1o'>Oil prices plunge as Iran says Strait &#x27;open&#x27;</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/04/17/iran-declares-hormuz-fully-open-until-ceasefire-ends-trump-insists-on-keeping-iranian-port-blockade'>Iran declares Hormuz &quot;fully open&quot; until ceasefire ends; Trump insists on keeping Iranian port blockade</a> - mercopress.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/wo